WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.251 --> 00:00:02.747
- Hello Believe Nation, we're
going to continue the experiment

00:00:02.747 --> 00:00:05.545
of my favorite top ten rules to success.

00:00:05.545 --> 00:00:07.341
Basically, all the videos that we've had

00:00:07.341 --> 00:00:08.920
from the past month, the top tens,

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:11.153
pulling out what my favorite rule was

00:00:11.153 --> 00:00:13.842
and mashing them together just for you.

00:00:13.842 --> 00:00:15.056
Let me know what you think,

00:00:15.056 --> 00:00:16.441
and as always, if you hear something

00:00:16.441 --> 00:00:17.720
that really resonates with you,

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.787
please leave it in the comments below.

00:00:19.787 --> 00:00:21.218
Put quotes around it so other people

00:00:21.218 --> 00:00:23.339
can be inspired as well, enjoy.

00:00:23.339 --> 00:00:26.640
(loud rushing)

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.557
♫ Woah

00:00:29.768 --> 00:00:31.768
♫ Woah

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.270
♫ I used to rule the world

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.031
♫ Seas would rise when I gave the word

00:00:40.031 --> 00:00:41.803
♫ Now in the morning I sleep

00:00:41.803 --> 00:00:43.200
- [Narrator] London,
just before Christmas,

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.494
the soaring melodies, the
thumping beat of the music

00:00:45.494 --> 00:00:48.704
and the quirky charisma of
lead singer Chris Martin

00:00:48.704 --> 00:00:50.954
had the crowd on it's feet.

00:00:52.531 --> 00:00:55.073
- We rely more on enthusiasm
than actual skill.

00:00:55.073 --> 00:00:57.490
(rock music)

00:00:58.946 --> 00:00:59.909
Whatever you do, do it enthusiastically

00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:01.504
and people will like it more.

00:01:01.504 --> 00:01:04.785
♫ Come back and sing to me

00:01:04.785 --> 00:01:05.911
I can't dance like Usher,

00:01:05.911 --> 00:01:07.564
I can't sing like Beyonce,

00:01:07.564 --> 00:01:09.784
I can't write songs like Elton John

00:01:09.784 --> 00:01:11.049
♫ Come on and sing it out

00:01:11.049 --> 00:01:12.895
♫ Now

00:01:12.895 --> 00:01:14.689
But we can do the best
we can with what we got.

00:01:14.689 --> 00:01:17.400
♫ Come on and sing it out

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.018
♫ For me

00:01:19.018 --> 00:01:20.808
♫ For me

00:01:20.808 --> 00:01:22.786
And so that's what we
do, we just go for it.

00:01:22.786 --> 00:01:23.702
(whooshing)

00:01:23.702 --> 00:01:25.463
- I really get motivated
when people tell me

00:01:25.463 --> 00:01:27.842
it'll never happen, it's like, you know.

00:01:27.842 --> 00:01:30.473
I mean it happens to me all
the time in my business,

00:01:30.473 --> 00:01:32.290
you got to understand guys,
I got to reinvent myself

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:34.809
every record, so that means
every three to six months

00:01:34.809 --> 00:01:36.131
I got to find myself again,

00:01:36.131 --> 00:01:37.201
because the last record,
or the new record,

00:01:37.201 --> 00:01:38.873
is not as big as the last record.

00:01:38.873 --> 00:01:42.343
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:42.343 --> 00:01:44.855
(laughter)

00:01:44.855 --> 00:01:45.803
But you guys don't understand,

00:01:45.803 --> 00:01:47.916
I got a guy that's sitting in an office

00:01:47.916 --> 00:01:49.280
that hasn't been to a club

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.057
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:51.057 --> 00:01:52.015
(audience laughing)

00:01:52.015 --> 00:01:54.831
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:54.831 --> 00:01:56.578
He don't understand none of that.

00:01:56.578 --> 00:01:58.108
Telling me what a hit record is.

00:01:58.108 --> 00:01:59.671
(laughter)

00:01:59.671 --> 00:02:01.077
You know?

00:02:01.077 --> 00:02:03.001
So you got to be able
to stick to your gut,

00:02:03.001 --> 00:02:04.546
stick to what you believe in,

00:02:04.546 --> 00:02:06.399
understand your worth, and go, okay,

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:08.879
well this is where you
build an infrastructure.

00:02:08.879 --> 00:02:11.259
At least in my business,
we build our own team,

00:02:11.259 --> 00:02:14.852
our own one stop shop
where if someone says no,

00:02:14.852 --> 00:02:16.436
we don't think it is, say don't worry,

00:02:16.436 --> 00:02:18.120
we'll check back in in a
month, and we'll see who,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.554
you know man, it'll just happen,

00:02:19.554 --> 00:02:21.139
it'll go, oh that'll never work.

00:02:21.139 --> 00:02:23.409
And in a month, (speaking
in foreign language)

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:25.172
You know this is really
starting to sound like a hit.

00:02:25.172 --> 00:02:26.781
(laughter)

00:02:26.781 --> 00:02:30.160
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:30.993
- [Interviewer] So yeah.

00:02:30.993 --> 00:02:31.826
- So that motivates me.

00:02:31.826 --> 00:02:33.915
That to me is what makes
me get up in the morning

00:02:33.915 --> 00:02:34.748
and go, what's next?

00:02:34.748 --> 00:02:35.581
(whooshing)

00:02:35.581 --> 00:02:37.195
- What were you like as a child?

00:02:37.195 --> 00:02:41.195
- I was a combination of
reclusive and obsessed

00:02:42.257 --> 00:02:44.455
with what I wanted to do.

00:02:44.455 --> 00:02:47.336
When I was two, I was
sitting in front of the TV

00:02:47.336 --> 00:02:49.048
drawing Fred Flintstones,
- Yeah.

00:02:49.048 --> 00:02:51.659
- And Woody Peckers, and at
age seven I'm running around

00:02:51.659 --> 00:02:53.576
like a crazy person doing voices,

00:02:53.576 --> 00:02:56.495
but at the same time I would
try to make animated films

00:02:56.495 --> 00:03:00.506
and try to figure out exactly
how that art form worked.

00:03:00.506 --> 00:03:02.650
- When did you do your first cartoon?

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.171
How old were you and what was it?

00:03:04.171 --> 00:03:05.622
- I was in seventh grade.

00:03:05.622 --> 00:03:08.314
It was called Space
Pirates, it was terrible.

00:03:08.314 --> 00:03:09.147
(slam)

00:03:09.147 --> 00:03:10.737
- I, as you know, I was
a bouncer for many years.

00:03:10.737 --> 00:03:13.721
I remember coming home one
night after a big fight,

00:03:13.721 --> 00:03:17.151
and I run up to the kitchen, I get ice,

00:03:17.151 --> 00:03:21.151
and my hand's swollen,
just another day at work.

00:03:22.271 --> 00:03:25.188
And I was feeling like, just bummed

00:03:28.483 --> 00:03:31.280
about the fact that I
was still doing this job

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.947
after all this time.

00:03:36.917 --> 00:03:41.074
At that one moment, I
was like, why are people,

00:03:41.074 --> 00:03:44.741
what's up, why do people
have so much anger?

00:03:45.761 --> 00:03:49.390
Why is this my daily life to
suppress everybody's anger

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:52.312
or check everybody's anger
that comes into a giant

00:03:52.312 --> 00:03:53.943
3,000 person nightclub?

00:03:53.943 --> 00:03:54.872
- Mhmm.

00:03:54.872 --> 00:03:57.552
- And my father, it was so simple,

00:03:57.552 --> 00:04:01.031
but my father said, "you
know Vin, I've traveled

00:04:01.031 --> 00:04:04.204
around the world. I've been to India,

00:04:04.204 --> 00:04:07.537
I've been to Russia, I've been to China,

00:04:09.192 --> 00:04:12.634
I've been all through South America,

00:04:12.634 --> 00:04:15.219
and I've realized something."

00:04:15.219 --> 00:04:16.469
I said, "what?"

00:04:17.592 --> 00:04:21.592
He said, "people by and
large, are good people."

00:04:28.633 --> 00:04:32.633
Sounded pretty simple,
and yet it meant so much.

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:36.963
And if you look at my Facebook page,

00:04:36.963 --> 00:04:41.068
or if you look at the way
that all that positive energy,

00:04:41.068 --> 00:04:45.640
I don't think there's a page
in the world, in history,

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:48.635
that harbors as much positive energy

00:04:48.635 --> 00:04:51.885
as one would see on this Facebook page.

00:04:53.814 --> 00:04:54.647
And it's really--

00:04:54.647 --> 00:04:55.552
- It's what you put out, too.

00:04:55.552 --> 00:04:56.601
- And it's really that.

00:04:56.601 --> 00:04:57.434
- Right.

00:04:58.959 --> 00:05:00.709
- I say that because,

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:05.871
if you believe that in your heart,

00:05:05.871 --> 00:05:08.693
and not everybody watching
this can say that,

00:05:08.693 --> 00:05:10.879
or is ready to say that, or is comfortable

00:05:10.879 --> 00:05:13.161
with themselves enough to say that,

00:05:13.161 --> 00:05:15.767
but if you really believe in your heart

00:05:15.767 --> 00:05:19.184
that people by and large are good people,

00:05:20.918 --> 00:05:24.825
it affects the way that
you address the world.

00:05:24.825 --> 00:05:27.658
As you see, post in and post out,

00:05:30.182 --> 00:05:33.099
I address a world that, in my soul,

00:05:34.507 --> 00:05:37.424
I feel is comprised of good people.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:39.505
(slam)

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:41.507
- Yeah, I've always been driven, you know,

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.761
even at a young age,
all I ever wanted to do

00:05:43.761 --> 00:05:46.743
was become a footballer, a soccer star.

00:05:46.743 --> 00:05:49.610
And that was my only ambition.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:52.420
I know it's different
these days with kids,

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.172
you know, obviously there's
fame, there's fortune,

00:05:55.172 --> 00:05:58.459
and it might be easy for me to say that

00:05:58.459 --> 00:06:01.593
because obviously I've done
very well out of the game,

00:06:01.593 --> 00:06:04.540
but I'd still be playing this game

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.194
even if I wasn't being paid for it.

00:06:07.194 --> 00:06:08.802
Not that I don't want
- Mission accomplished.

00:06:08.802 --> 00:06:09.921
- To be paid for it.

00:06:09.921 --> 00:06:11.001
(audience laughing)
(interviewer laughing)

00:06:11.001 --> 00:06:14.334
But I still would be playing this sport.

00:06:15.694 --> 00:06:16.605
(slam)

00:06:16.605 --> 00:06:20.355
- The notion that we
are not enough is a lie.

00:06:21.313 --> 00:06:23.645
And I think more young
people need to know that.

00:06:23.645 --> 00:06:25.571
That like, when you have that thought

00:06:25.571 --> 00:06:30.564
that you're not enough,
that that's based on a lie

00:06:30.564 --> 00:06:33.682
that somebody said to you, or a lie

00:06:33.682 --> 00:06:35.378
that somebody did to you.

00:06:35.378 --> 00:06:37.137
Like, somebody said or
did something to you

00:06:37.137 --> 00:06:39.686
to make you feel like you're not enough,

00:06:39.686 --> 00:06:42.065
and that's not true.

00:06:42.065 --> 00:06:45.732
That you are everything
that you need to be,

00:06:46.911 --> 00:06:49.262
you have everything that you need to have

00:06:49.262 --> 00:06:51.503
to be the person that you're meant to be

00:06:51.503 --> 00:06:55.040
and to be the best version
of who you're meant to be.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:57.708
It may mean that you
have to work really hard,

00:06:57.708 --> 00:07:00.536
and it may mean that you
have to ask for help,

00:07:00.536 --> 00:07:03.036
but you are perfectly blessed.

00:07:04.768 --> 00:07:08.124
You know, you are exactly
who you're supposed to be

00:07:08.124 --> 00:07:11.077
to be joyful in this life.

00:07:11.077 --> 00:07:12.900
So don't be afraid to work hard,

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.469
and don't be afraid to ask for help

00:07:14.469 --> 00:07:16.917
so you can get there,
because that's your truth.

00:07:16.917 --> 00:07:17.917
(slam)

00:07:17.917 --> 00:07:19.175
- You have to be authentic.

00:07:19.175 --> 00:07:21.836
I mean, you've got no
hope if you try to pretend

00:07:21.836 --> 00:07:26.027
to be Reed Hastings, or Mark
Zuckerberg, or Bill Gates,

00:07:26.027 --> 00:07:28.517
or whoever you want to
think you're CEOs are

00:07:28.517 --> 00:07:31.912
that you've met, you know, that is crazy.

00:07:31.912 --> 00:07:33.904
And you got to really get
comfortable with yourself,

00:07:33.904 --> 00:07:35.877
your strengths and weaknesses.

00:07:35.877 --> 00:07:40.044
People are inspired by
authenticity, and I'm just surprised

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.282
how many people feel like they're acting,

00:07:45.282 --> 00:07:46.782
and or try to act.

00:07:47.697 --> 00:07:51.434
And you may be a really quiet
person, and that's fine,

00:07:51.434 --> 00:07:53.993
but if you have that fierce will,

00:07:53.993 --> 00:07:58.160
that desire for the institution,
the company to be great,

00:07:59.137 --> 00:08:02.552
and your own role in that is secondary,

00:08:02.552 --> 00:08:05.732
and you authentically express
that in whatever ways you do,

00:08:05.732 --> 00:08:09.274
then you'll be greatly accepted, okay?

00:08:09.274 --> 00:08:12.095
And so that's all it
takes is to be authentic

00:08:12.095 --> 00:08:13.859
and not try to be something else,

00:08:13.859 --> 00:08:16.356
and to fundamentally care, at your core,

00:08:16.356 --> 00:08:18.568
about what's good for the company.

00:08:18.568 --> 00:08:21.261
And if everybody knows,
and you act in the notion

00:08:21.261 --> 00:08:23.855
of what's good as best as
your judgment can tell,

00:08:23.855 --> 00:08:26.428
what's good for the
company, they will see that.

00:08:26.428 --> 00:08:28.988
And people will follow you,
and they won't be cynical.

00:08:28.988 --> 00:08:31.476
It's when you hide things,
it's when you pretend.

00:08:31.476 --> 00:08:32.309
(slam)

00:08:32.309 --> 00:08:35.228
- Everybody has their own
sense of accomplishment,

00:08:35.228 --> 00:08:37.630
you know, some it's
opening weekend box office,

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:38.833
or winning Academy Awards.

00:08:38.833 --> 00:08:42.850
To me, it is being in
an audience anonymously

00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:44.423
and watching people watch my movies,

00:08:44.423 --> 00:08:46.664
and seeing how we're entertaining people.

00:08:46.664 --> 00:08:50.111
The first true experience
I ever had of that

00:08:50.111 --> 00:08:53.861
was about five days
after Toy Story came out.

00:08:54.865 --> 00:08:56.927
It took four years for
me to make Toy Story,

00:08:56.927 --> 00:09:01.094
all of us at Pixar, and I
was flying with my family

00:09:02.053 --> 00:09:05.617
and we were changing planes in
the Dallas For Worth Airport.

00:09:05.617 --> 00:09:08.032
I got off the plane, and
there was a little boy,

00:09:08.032 --> 00:09:10.250
about four years old, with his mother,

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:13.833
and he was holding a
Woody the Cowboy doll.

00:09:15.068 --> 00:09:16.860
And it's the first time I'd
seen one of my characters

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:20.181
outside of Pixar, and I was so stunned,

00:09:20.181 --> 00:09:21.985
my boy's, "Dad, look, look, look."

00:09:21.985 --> 00:09:26.190
The look on his face, he
couldn't wait to show his father,

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:29.151
who was getting off the plane, his toy.

00:09:29.151 --> 00:09:32.164
And I realized at that
moment that that character

00:09:32.164 --> 00:09:36.575
that I, with the help of
everybody at Pixar, created

00:09:36.575 --> 00:09:40.958
over four years, no longer
belong to me, it belonged to him.

00:09:40.958 --> 00:09:44.541
And to be honest, it's
so much in my memory

00:09:46.576 --> 00:09:48.514
the look on that little boy's face,

00:09:48.514 --> 00:09:50.568
I think about that little
boy every single day

00:09:50.568 --> 00:09:52.847
working at Pixar, and
that's why we do what we do.

00:09:52.847 --> 00:09:53.680
(slam)

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.731
- Well, certainly every product we do

00:09:55.731 --> 00:09:59.381
is absolutely as capable
as it can possibly be.

00:09:59.381 --> 00:10:02.755
You know, there's no holding back.

00:10:02.755 --> 00:10:06.755
The people at Microsoft
come into work every day

00:10:07.659 --> 00:10:09.928
building the best products they can.

00:10:09.928 --> 00:10:10.806
And they're very proud.

00:10:10.806 --> 00:10:12.502
You know, you go out, go into schools

00:10:12.502 --> 00:10:14.278
and see how kids are using this stuff.

00:10:14.278 --> 00:10:16.523
Go and ask people about
how their jobs have changed

00:10:16.523 --> 00:10:18.232
because of the personal computer.

00:10:18.232 --> 00:10:19.526
You know,
- [Interviewer] Sure.

00:10:19.526 --> 00:10:21.296
- We're sitting there,
listening to our customers

00:10:21.296 --> 00:10:23.472
saying how they'd like
to make things better.

00:10:23.472 --> 00:10:26.429
So, we do absolutely our best job,

00:10:26.429 --> 00:10:29.122
and in fact, that's why we've
been successful, you know.

00:10:29.122 --> 00:10:31.390
Anybody who holds back in this business

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:33.167
isn't going to be around for long,

00:10:33.167 --> 00:10:35.043
because this is a business

00:10:35.043 --> 00:10:39.488
where you always have to be
moving at a very rapid pace.

00:10:39.488 --> 00:10:40.321
(slam)

00:10:40.321 --> 00:10:42.995
- As yourself, what if no one
ever bought any of my work?

00:10:42.995 --> 00:10:45.806
What if I'm never going to sell a book,

00:10:45.806 --> 00:10:48.151
or publish a book, or a short story?

00:10:48.151 --> 00:10:51.068
Are you going to continue to write?

00:10:51.952 --> 00:10:54.302
And if the answer is
yes, because I have to,

00:10:54.302 --> 00:10:55.766
then yeah, you are a writer.

00:10:55.766 --> 00:10:58.014
And whether you'll sell
or not, I can't know,

00:10:58.014 --> 00:11:00.735
but if there's stories in
you that have to get out,

00:11:00.735 --> 00:11:02.680
you want to put them down on paper,

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:06.232
whether you ever get the
affirmation of a contract

00:11:06.232 --> 00:11:09.397
or a book in your hand, or
being on the best seller list,

00:11:09.397 --> 00:11:11.143
if you still have to tell your stories,

00:11:11.143 --> 00:11:12.924
then you're a writer.

00:11:12.924 --> 00:11:15.654
If you can give it up, then
it probably would be an act

00:11:15.654 --> 00:11:19.509
of sanity to learn accounting
or something like that.

00:11:19.509 --> 00:11:20.425
(slam)

00:11:20.425 --> 00:11:22.499
- In the long history of the world,

00:11:22.499 --> 00:11:26.582
only a few generations
have been granted the role

00:11:28.885 --> 00:11:33.679
of defending freedom in
it's hour of maximum danger.

00:11:33.679 --> 00:11:37.765
I do not shrink from this
responsibility, I welcome it.

00:11:37.765 --> 00:11:41.932
(audience cheering)
(audience applauding)

00:11:43.185 --> 00:11:47.352
I do not believe that any
of us would exchange places

00:11:48.457 --> 00:11:52.934
with any other people
or any other generation.

00:11:52.934 --> 00:11:55.851
The energy, the faith, the devotion

00:11:56.754 --> 00:11:59.421
which we bring to this endeavor.

00:12:00.394 --> 00:12:03.209
We'll light our country
and all who serve it,

00:12:03.209 --> 00:12:07.376
and the glow from that fire
can surely light the world.

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:12.050
And so my fellow Americans,

00:12:12.050 --> 00:12:15.467
ask not what your country can do for you,

00:12:17.635 --> 00:12:20.114
ask what you can do for your country.

00:12:20.114 --> 00:12:24.281
(audience cheering)
(audience applauding)

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:30.261
My fellow citizens of the world,

00:12:30.261 --> 00:12:33.344
ask not what America will do for you,

00:12:35.694 --> 00:12:39.095
but what together we can
do for the freedom of man.

00:12:39.095 --> 00:12:43.262
(audience cheering)
(audience applauding)

00:12:44.498 --> 00:12:48.986
Finally, whether you
are citizens of America,

00:12:48.986 --> 00:12:53.069
or citizens of the world,
ask of us here the same

00:12:54.584 --> 00:12:57.917
high standards of strength and sacrifice

00:12:59.837 --> 00:13:01.504
which we ask of you.

00:13:02.478 --> 00:13:06.145
With a good conscience
our only sure reward,

00:13:08.235 --> 00:13:12.306
with history the final judge of our deeds,

00:13:12.306 --> 00:13:15.723
let us go forth to lead the land we love,

00:13:16.561 --> 00:13:19.311
asking His blessing and His help,

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:23.664
but knowing that here on Earth,

00:13:23.664 --> 00:13:26.824
God's work must truly be our own.

00:13:26.824 --> 00:13:27.657
(slam)

00:13:27.657 --> 00:13:30.731
- I see myself as a pioneer in the game.

00:13:30.731 --> 00:13:33.256
I see Drake as a pioneer in the game,

00:13:33.256 --> 00:13:35.669
people like Lil Wayne,
there's only certain people

00:13:35.669 --> 00:13:39.252
who really come and
define your generation,

00:13:41.241 --> 00:13:43.404
other than being a part
of your generation.

00:13:43.404 --> 00:13:46.379
And it's cool to be a part of it as well,

00:13:46.379 --> 00:13:49.307
but this is how I see myself, as a leader

00:13:49.307 --> 00:13:53.693
and somebody who changes
things and doesn't just follow.

00:13:53.693 --> 00:13:54.526
(slam)

00:13:54.526 --> 00:13:56.134
- So you've sort of arrived
at these different approaches

00:13:56.134 --> 00:13:57.481
