00:14:08.724 --> 00:14:10.374
I could actually be really good at this.

00:14:10.374 --> 00:14:12.520
Or, wow, I just got
recognition for this piece.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:15.014
I mean, where there any
sort of defining moments

00:14:15.014 --> 00:14:16.872
in your career as a writer?

00:14:16.872 --> 00:14:18.189
- Yeah, I think, I mean, I don't know.

00:14:18.189 --> 00:14:19.536
I mean, the things that I remember,

00:14:19.536 --> 00:14:20.802
I remember like I was,

00:14:20.802 --> 00:14:23.993
by the way, the best way
to get started in anything

00:14:23.993 --> 00:14:25.487
is to to be willing to work for free.

00:14:25.487 --> 00:14:26.320
- Yeah.

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:28.679
- Internships, to me,
are the path to anything.

00:14:28.679 --> 00:14:32.305
I really think being, there's
definitely a lot of people

00:14:32.305 --> 00:14:34.901
who are entitled, basically,
I have so much to say.

00:14:34.901 --> 00:14:35.734
- Yeah.

00:14:35.734 --> 00:14:37.075
- But anyway, if you want to live

00:14:37.075 --> 00:14:37.908
in your passion,
(laughter)

00:14:37.908 --> 00:14:39.079
whatever your passion is,

00:14:39.079 --> 00:14:41.130
you have to be willing
to not make money at it.

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:42.332
If you want to do what you love,

00:14:42.332 --> 00:14:44.805
you have a choice, you can choose money,

00:14:44.805 --> 00:14:46.284
or you can choose what you love.

00:14:46.284 --> 00:14:48.489
Joseph Campbell has a great
quote that I always use,

00:14:48.489 --> 00:14:49.646
I don't know if you guys know this--

00:14:49.646 --> 00:14:52.421
- Joseph Campbell, so the
hero with a thousand faces.

00:14:52.421 --> 00:14:54.383
- Yeah the great professor of mythology.

00:14:54.383 --> 00:14:57.161
But he said the, have you guys heard this?

00:14:57.161 --> 00:14:59.502
The insecure way is the secure way.

00:14:59.502 --> 00:15:00.335
- [Audience Member] Mhm.

00:15:00.335 --> 00:15:01.168
- You guys have heard that quote?

00:15:01.168 --> 00:15:02.956
And what it means basically is, you know,

00:15:02.956 --> 00:15:05.510
my parents, and I'm sure like
most people's parents say,

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:08.197
get a good job, try and make
some money, make a good living.

00:15:08.197 --> 00:15:09.030
- Yep.

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:10.143
- Because they think that's secure,

00:15:10.143 --> 00:15:11.164
if you make go and make money.

00:15:11.164 --> 00:15:12.528
But if you do a job and you make money,

00:15:12.528 --> 00:15:13.397
and you lose that job,

00:15:13.397 --> 00:15:14.230
- Yep.

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:15.097
- Then yo lose the money,
and you have nothing.

00:15:15.097 --> 00:15:15.930
- Right, right, right.

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:17.149
- What he says, if you
choose your passion,

00:15:17.149 --> 00:15:17.982
- Yeah.

00:15:17.982 --> 00:15:19.013
- Actually doesn't matter
whether you make money

00:15:19.013 --> 00:15:20.574
or you lose money, because
you're always going to have

00:15:20.574 --> 00:15:22.563
your passion and be happy, so
it doesn't actually matter.

00:15:22.563 --> 00:15:23.396
- And that's your safety net.

00:15:23.396 --> 00:15:24.229
- Right.

00:15:24.229 --> 00:15:27.168
But the challenge of that is
that it might be eight years

00:15:27.168 --> 00:15:30.547
or four years before you're
actually making a living at it.

00:15:30.547 --> 00:15:31.380
- Yep.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:32.509
- Versus something else.

00:15:32.509 --> 00:15:33.421
(slam)

00:15:33.421 --> 00:15:37.908
- What about the business
of being what you are now?

00:15:37.908 --> 00:15:39.624
The musician, the songwriter.

00:15:39.624 --> 00:15:42.248
I mean, did you display and early talent

00:15:42.248 --> 00:15:43.480
for singing?

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:45.623
- I didn't actually, I got
thrown out of the choir.

00:15:45.623 --> 00:15:46.456
(audience laughing)

00:15:46.456 --> 00:15:47.289
- [Michael] Did you?

00:15:47.289 --> 00:15:49.800
- I was thrown out of
the choir for, I think,

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:51.263
as far as I know, it was for talking.

00:15:51.263 --> 00:15:52.391
(audience laughing)

00:15:52.391 --> 00:15:55.067
And I always remember my
dad used to say to me,

00:15:55.067 --> 00:15:56.600
you know, because I used to say dad,

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:59.264
that when I was like
sixteen, seventeen, eighteen,

00:15:59.264 --> 00:16:01.776
and my dad was threatening
to throw me out of the house

00:16:01.776 --> 00:16:04.800
if I didn't get a job within
a certain amount of time.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:06.341
So obviously I went out and got jobs,

00:16:06.341 --> 00:16:08.330
but I was saying I want
to be a singer, you know,

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:11.583
I think I'm going to be rich and famous.

00:16:11.583 --> 00:16:12.561
And he would say, you know,

00:16:12.561 --> 00:16:14.965
"everybody wants to be a pop
singer, everybody wants,"

00:16:14.965 --> 00:16:16.470
and sat there, and I would say, "no, Dad,

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:19.413
everybody wants to be a
pop singer when they're 12,

00:16:19.413 --> 00:16:20.758
you know, when they're 12.
(audience laughing)

00:16:20.758 --> 00:16:24.295
This is my whole life,
this is what I want to do."

00:16:24.295 --> 00:16:27.343
But, I've lost my train of thought.

00:16:27.343 --> 00:16:28.176
(audience laughing)

00:16:28.176 --> 00:16:30.118
- Was it to be rich and
famous, your ambition?

00:16:30.118 --> 00:16:30.951
I mean, was that it?

00:16:30.951 --> 00:16:33.276
- No, I think I had two
very, very strong ambitions

00:16:33.276 --> 00:16:36.370
and they've always fought
against one another, always.

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:38.924
And one was a passion for music,

00:16:38.924 --> 00:16:42.497
and an ability to
communicate through music,

00:16:42.497 --> 00:16:45.431
and really a sense of
purpose through music.

00:16:45.431 --> 00:16:49.755
And two, was this desire
to be, and rich an famous

00:16:49.755 --> 00:16:51.984
is not right, because actually
rich never came into it.

00:16:51.984 --> 00:16:54.154
I actually realized about six months ago,

00:16:54.154 --> 00:16:55.863
someone put a question to me,

00:16:55.863 --> 00:16:58.545
and I actually realized that at no point

00:16:58.545 --> 00:17:01.006
during my early life when I was,

00:17:01.006 --> 00:17:04.673
in all my ambitions,
and even when I started

00:17:05.859 --> 00:17:10.196
to realize my ambitions, at no
point did it ever occur to me

00:17:10.196 --> 00:17:12.451
that one of the byproducts
of this would be

00:17:12.451 --> 00:17:14.161
that I could buy whatever I wanted,

00:17:14.161 --> 00:17:16.735
or you know, live in a big
house, have a flashy car,

00:17:16.735 --> 00:17:18.527
all the things that are very
- [Michael] Yes.

00:17:18.527 --> 00:17:19.677
- Pleasant, you know.

00:17:19.677 --> 00:17:21.356
But it really hadn't occurred to me,

00:17:21.356 --> 00:17:22.753
it never occurred to me.

00:17:22.753 --> 00:17:23.769
So I didn't want to be rich,

00:17:23.769 --> 00:17:25.650
I just wanted to be filthily famous.

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:26.483
(slam)

00:17:26.483 --> 00:17:28.840
- There's a lot of work
that we're responsible for

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:31.013
that other people can't do, you know.

00:17:31.013 --> 00:17:33.062
There's a saying that says,

00:17:33.062 --> 00:17:35.221
you can lead a horse to the water,

00:17:35.221 --> 00:17:36.054
- [Interviewer] But you can't--

00:17:36.054 --> 00:17:37.645
- But you can't make them drink.

00:17:37.645 --> 00:17:40.879
In the presence of common
sense and rational thinking,

00:17:40.879 --> 00:17:44.399
even in throes of your despair,
it is your responsibility

00:17:44.399 --> 00:17:48.566
if you are willing to still
commit to saving your own life.

00:17:50.477 --> 00:17:54.493
To hear that, and to walk
toward that voice, you know.

00:17:54.493 --> 00:17:58.660
So, you know, I want to recognize
personal responsibility.

00:18:01.431 --> 00:18:03.729
And in what we try to do here today,

00:18:03.729 --> 00:18:06.452
one of the things that makes body building

00:18:06.452 --> 00:18:11.079
a significant vehicle for
therapy, and maybe even a tool

00:18:11.079 --> 00:18:14.716
towards personal
development, it is just that.

00:18:14.716 --> 00:18:17.702
You know, in order to be
able to do this effectively,

00:18:17.702 --> 00:18:20.750
you have to recognize your
personal responsibility.

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:23.831
So, with that said, you know,

00:18:23.831 --> 00:18:27.664
I want to submit to you
that the word believe,

00:18:28.668 --> 00:18:30.252
believe is work.

00:18:30.252 --> 00:18:33.252
You can substitute work for believe.

00:18:34.751 --> 00:18:35.686
You know, because when I'm working,

00:18:35.686 --> 00:18:37.176
what are you working for?

00:18:37.176 --> 00:18:38.832
If you're working without a belief

00:18:38.832 --> 00:18:40.719
and the idea that you're
going to get paid,

00:18:40.719 --> 00:18:43.151
then what is your work for?

00:18:43.151 --> 00:18:44.521
What is the purpose?

00:18:44.521 --> 00:18:48.688
So, I believe that, you
know, to talk about believing

00:18:49.798 --> 00:18:51.694
is to talk about being
willing to do the work.

00:18:51.694 --> 00:18:52.527
(slam)

00:18:52.527 --> 00:18:54.703
- Whatever you going to do,
just do it for a reason.

00:18:54.703 --> 00:18:55.769
You know what I'm
saying, don't be the fool

00:18:55.769 --> 00:18:56.717
that just sit back and do it

00:18:56.717 --> 00:18:58.111
because somebody else is doing it.

00:18:58.111 --> 00:18:59.341
Because if all that you
going to do is follow

00:18:59.341 --> 00:19:01.918
in somebody else's footsteps,
you're just going to be a shadow.

00:19:01.918 --> 00:19:03.765
Who wants to be a shadow
for the rest of their life,

00:19:03.765 --> 00:19:04.918
you know what I'm saying?

00:19:04.918 --> 00:19:06.621
If you got a little something
that you know how to do,

00:19:06.621 --> 00:19:08.612
progress at it, keep trying,

00:19:08.612 --> 00:19:11.015
God knows what could happen,
you know what I'm saying?

00:19:11.015 --> 00:19:13.343
That's what I did, just kept on rapping,

00:19:13.343 --> 00:19:14.961
just happened to get to the right areas,

00:19:14.961 --> 00:19:15.794
you know what I'm saying?

00:19:15.794 --> 00:19:16.627
(slam)

00:19:16.627 --> 00:19:18.014
- Martin Luther King tried
to commit suicide twice.

00:19:18.014 --> 00:19:19.566
You guys seen Osine here?

00:19:19.566 --> 00:19:21.966
JFK went through periods of deep illness,

00:19:21.966 --> 00:19:24.709
deeply, deeply ill where
people thought he would die.

00:19:24.709 --> 00:19:25.789
These types of things, right?

00:19:25.789 --> 00:19:27.652
Winston Churchill went through
deep, deep depressions.

00:19:27.652 --> 00:19:28.900
So you can become one of those people,

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:30.064
doesn't mean you're going
to become on of them

00:19:30.064 --> 00:19:31.135
because that's, I mean those guys

00:19:31.135 --> 00:19:33.869
are in their own universe, but you know.

00:19:33.869 --> 00:19:35.814
You can become somebody of great power

00:19:35.814 --> 00:19:39.390
if you've gone through that,
because you've been hardened.

00:19:39.390 --> 00:19:40.650
Do you guys see what I mean here?

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:41.991
If you've been through that kind of bull,

00:19:41.991 --> 00:19:44.307
then if you've conditioned yourself

00:19:44.307 --> 00:19:46.141
to pull yourself out
of something like that,

00:19:46.141 --> 00:19:48.485
if the world gets all up,

00:19:48.485 --> 00:19:50.158
all the other people,
they're used to happiness.

00:19:50.158 --> 00:19:51.231
They're like, woah, happy,

00:19:51.231 --> 00:19:53.046
and they're like, my happy
world's falling apart!

00:19:53.046 --> 00:19:55.487
My happy world, it's falling apart!

00:19:55.487 --> 00:19:57.737
Gets all, you're just like,

00:19:59.324 --> 00:20:01.407
it's all good baby, hell yeah.

00:20:01.407 --> 00:20:03.620
You know, you don't care,
because you're adjusted to it.

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:06.203
You've taken conscious control.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:07.913
Do you guys see what I mean?

00:20:07.913 --> 00:20:08.746
- [Audience Memmber] Mm.

00:20:08.746 --> 00:20:09.579
- Okay?

00:20:09.579 --> 00:20:10.480
So there's benefits.

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:12.200
Being a hard case newbie
means that you can be better

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:14.660
than any of the guys that had it easily.

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:16.226
It'll be harder to get,

00:20:16.226 --> 00:20:19.351
but you can excel to a higher level

00:20:19.351 --> 00:20:21.760
than all the people that
were naturally good,

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:23.177
if you choose to.

00:20:24.105 --> 00:20:26.961
But for you, it will
be a life of commitment

00:20:26.961 --> 00:20:29.040
in the same way that a guy
that wants to get kind of big

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:31.487
in the gym, if he's going
to be big in the gym

00:20:31.487 --> 00:20:32.985
and he's a hard gainer little skinny dude,

00:20:32.985 --> 00:20:35.168
he's going to have a
higher commitment level

00:20:35.168 --> 00:20:36.001
to get to that level,

00:20:36.001 --> 00:20:36.962
you're going to have a
higher commitment level,

00:20:36.962 --> 00:20:38.210
you're not going to get
to average as easily.

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:40.091
And sometimes even
striving to stay at average

00:20:40.091 --> 00:20:42.442
won't work for you because you slip back,

00:20:42.442 --> 00:20:44.628
so you have to really commit, you have to,

00:20:44.628 --> 00:20:46.377
you have to take your
foot off the parking brake

00:20:46.377 --> 00:20:48.578
and you have to burn
the boats, as they say,

00:20:48.578 --> 00:20:49.866
and just go for it.

00:20:49.866 --> 00:20:51.131
As a hard case newbie, if you do that,

00:20:51.131 --> 00:20:53.475
you can slowly build
those willpower muscles,

00:20:53.475 --> 00:20:56.308
slowly re-acclimate
your body to positivity,

00:20:56.308 --> 00:20:58.992
build up friends, build up abundance,

00:20:58.992 --> 00:21:00.705
build an amazing situation for yourself,

00:21:00.705 --> 00:21:01.930
do amazing, amazing things.

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:02.763
(slam)

00:21:02.763 --> 00:21:04.813
- I continually learn from my mistakes

00:21:04.813 --> 00:21:06.892
and other people's mistakes.

00:21:06.892 --> 00:21:09.388
See, entrepreneurs think
that, hey, to create

00:21:09.388 --> 00:21:11.071
that billion dollar company,
or hundred million dollar

00:21:11.071 --> 00:21:12.361
company, or even million dollar company,

00:21:12.361 --> 00:21:14.387
you have to be really smart.

00:21:14.387 --> 00:21:18.834
And sure, brains is part of
it, luck is a huge component,

00:21:18.834 --> 00:21:20.146
but what I've learned over the years

00:21:20.146 --> 00:21:22.810
is everyone, no matter how smart you are,

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:24.772
you're going to fail many times,

00:21:24.772 --> 00:21:26.154
you're going to make mistakes.

00:21:26.154 --> 00:21:27.634
Just look at Mark Zuckerberg,

00:21:27.634 --> 00:21:29.345
Facebook worked out.

00:21:29.345 --> 00:21:32.787
His version of Snapchat that
was copy-cat did not work out.

00:21:32.787 --> 00:21:33.642
Right?

00:21:33.642 --> 00:21:36.231
So there's other areas
in which Facebook failed,

00:21:36.231 --> 00:21:38.576
but Facebook overall as
a company has done great.

00:21:38.576 --> 00:21:40.043
Just because you're smart

00:21:40.043 --> 00:21:41.552
doesn't mean you're
always going to succeed

00:21:41.552 --> 00:21:43.673
with everything you try or touch.

00:21:43.673 --> 00:21:47.602
But, every time you do something,
you will learn new things,

00:21:47.602 --> 00:21:50.638
you'll make mistakes, and if
you learn from these mistakes

00:21:50.638 --> 00:21:53.482
and avoid making them over and over again,

00:21:53.482 --> 00:21:56.211
you'll actually increase
your odds of succeeding

00:21:56.211 --> 00:21:57.691
as time goes on.

00:21:57.691 --> 00:21:59.416
And the best way to do
this is not just to learn

00:21:59.416 --> 00:22:01.633
from your mistakes, but it's also to learn

00:22:01.633 --> 00:22:03.364
from the mistakes of others.

00:22:03.364 --> 00:22:05.429
Rockefeller, Carnagie, all these people

00:22:05.429 --> 00:22:08.906
who were entrepreneurs years
ago in the 1800s, 1900s,

00:22:08.906 --> 00:22:13.360
they themselves used business
principles and concepts

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:16.041
that are still applicable
in today's world.

00:22:16.041 --> 00:22:17.986
Sure, we have the internet,
but the same strategies

00:22:17.986 --> 00:22:20.610
and concepts they they used
are still applicable, right?

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:22.387
So learn from their mistakes,

00:22:22.387 --> 00:22:24.703
learn what worked for them,
what didn't work for them

00:22:24.703 --> 00:22:27.780
and why, and bring that into
what you're doing today.

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:28.613
(slam)

00:22:28.613 --> 00:22:29.446
(dramatic music)

00:22:29.446 --> 00:22:31.756
- In the very beginning of
me getting into the industry,

00:22:31.756 --> 00:22:33.764
I would, you know, be out and about,

00:22:33.764 --> 00:22:36.847
and I would hear girls, or actresses,

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:41.415
or musicians, or whatever, you know,

00:22:41.415 --> 00:22:44.155
they would say, oh no I don't
want to be in a relationship

00:22:44.155 --> 00:22:45.398
because I don't want to have to choose

00:22:45.398 --> 00:22:48.706
between being in a relationship
and having a career.

00:22:48.706 --> 00:22:50.657
And I thought to myself, pfft,

00:22:50.657 --> 00:22:53.850
well when I get to know that,

00:22:53.850 --> 00:22:58.324
when I find that person that's
going to be my, you know,

00:22:58.324 --> 00:23:01.425
life partner, I won't ever have to choose,

00:23:01.425 --> 00:23:03.283
because they'll be accepting of me

00:23:03.283 --> 00:23:05.733
completely for who I am.

00:23:05.733 --> 00:23:09.459
Not me being able to just
like do whatever I want

00:23:09.459 --> 00:23:12.634
necessarily, but they won't be threatened

00:23:12.634 --> 00:23:15.551
or have weird motives, or whatever.

00:23:16.791 --> 00:23:20.402
They will be accepting
of me, the things I love,

00:23:20.402 --> 00:23:23.200
the ambition, what I aspire to be,

00:23:23.200 --> 00:23:27.588
what I want to create, they'll
be accepting and supportive.

00:23:27.588 --> 00:23:31.755
And then I started to realize,
oh my God, that's not true.

00:23:33.043 --> 00:23:36.917
Like that fairytale that I had
is not true for me right now.

00:23:36.917 --> 00:23:39.318
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
but you still miss him.

00:23:39.318 --> 00:23:41.729
- Yeah (sighing).

00:23:41.729 --> 00:23:42.979
I don't want...

00:23:48.608 --> 00:23:51.400
(slow music)

00:23:51.400 --> 00:23:54.024
- [Announcer] Okay again
everyone, gates are closed,

00:23:54.024 --> 00:23:57.931
gates are closed (drowned out by music).

00:23:57.931 --> 00:23:59.583
- I'll tell you the truth (sniffing),

00:23:59.583 --> 00:24:01.833
is, I mean, I'm a romantic,

00:24:04.379 --> 00:24:07.591
and I kind of believed in this fairytale.

00:24:07.591 --> 00:24:08.972
(sniffing)

00:24:08.972 --> 00:24:11.312
I mean (sighs), and in some ways,

00:24:11.312 --> 00:24:13.459
I think that's always
been to my advantage,

00:24:13.459 --> 00:24:16.436
because like, if you can
believe in something great,

00:24:16.436 --> 00:24:19.363
I feel like you can
achieve something great.

00:24:19.363 --> 00:24:20.443
(footsteps)

00:24:20.443 --> 00:24:23.328
I have that same belief
system with everything

00:24:23.328 --> 00:24:27.495
from career to my life, my
personal life, everything.

00:24:30.896 --> 00:24:34.357
And I will do everything
it takes to not fail,

00:24:34.357 --> 00:24:38.797
and I did everything it
took, but it still failed.

00:24:38.797 --> 00:24:40.772
- [Announcer] 321.
(slow music)

00:24:40.772 --> 00:24:44.207
- The bad that comes along
with the good is a journey

00:24:44.207 --> 00:24:46.060
and I learned so much from that journey.

00:24:46.060 --> 00:24:46.893
(slam)

00:24:46.893 --> 00:24:50.069
- I mean, you are
unforgivingly who you are.

00:24:50.069 --> 00:24:51.196
Have you always been like that?

00:24:51.196 --> 00:24:52.243
- No, no.

00:24:52.243 --> 00:24:53.171
- [Interviewer] What happened?

00:24:53.171 --> 00:24:54.192
- Like when I was in college.

00:24:54.192 --> 00:24:55.025
- [Interviewer] What were you like,

00:24:55.025 --> 00:24:56.252
like were you ever insecure?

00:24:56.252 --> 00:24:58.516
- I still am insecure, like, I mean,

00:24:58.516 --> 00:24:59.887
there's probably people
snickering backstage

00:24:59.887 --> 00:25:01.443
right now at that question, like (laughs).

00:25:01.443 --> 00:25:03.589
(audience laughing)

00:25:03.589 --> 00:25:05.339
When I was in college I
didn't want to come out

00:25:05.339 --> 00:25:06.828
as a progressive because I would eliminate

00:25:06.828 --> 00:25:10.735
like half of my future
employers in Washington D.C.

00:25:10.735 --> 00:25:12.316
I was like, look, I got
to look neutral here

00:25:12.316 --> 00:25:14.566
so I can get a job someday.

00:25:16.745 --> 00:25:19.241
I found, progressively, and you know,

00:25:19.241 --> 00:25:20.738
some of these lessons were forced upon me.

00:25:20.738 --> 00:25:22.509
I mentioned earlier, so
not to repeat a story,

00:25:22.509 --> 00:25:26.399
I was at Google, I am working on this team

00:25:26.399 --> 00:25:27.820
and this wireless stuff.

00:25:27.820 --> 00:25:29.585
It was incredible, my responsibility.

00:25:29.585 --> 00:25:32.566
Larry had given me, Larry
Page had given me billions

00:25:32.566 --> 00:25:34.574
of dollars to go take on
the wireless carriers,

00:25:34.574 --> 00:25:36.728
and I was in over my head.

00:25:36.728 --> 00:25:38.353
I didn't have a background in this stuff,

00:25:38.353 --> 00:25:40.824
and I was leaning fast,
and I was pretty good at it

00:25:40.824 --> 00:25:43.385
but one day a decision came up
and I didn't know what to do,

00:25:43.385 --> 00:25:46.312
and my work partner wrote in the notes,

00:25:46.312 --> 00:25:47.912
"Chris doesn't know
what to do about this."

00:25:47.912 --> 00:25:50.658
And at Google, the notes are
published to the whole company.

00:25:50.658 --> 00:25:53.032
So, I see the notes get
published, and I'm like,

00:25:53.032 --> 00:25:54.625
"what did you do, you
just ruined my career!

00:25:54.625 --> 00:25:57.920
Everyone's going to know,
like, I'm smoke and mirrors."

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:00.976
And I thought it was over for me.

00:26:00.976 --> 00:26:03.431
And then the next meeting,
some new engineers

00:26:03.431 --> 00:26:05.149
who didn't report to me,
and Larry Page himself

00:26:05.149 --> 00:26:07.992
showed up with ideas on what
to do to solve this problem,

00:26:07.992 --> 00:26:11.198
and I was like, oh my God,
I just became more powerful.

00:26:11.198 --> 00:26:12.031
I now have influence.

00:26:12.031 --> 00:26:13.152
I have more bodies working for me

00:26:13.152 --> 00:26:14.928
who don't even report to me.

00:26:14.928 --> 00:26:18.599
I mean, I wish I could say
it was an enlightened choice

00:26:18.599 --> 00:26:20.241
I'd made, it was totally forced upon me

00:26:20.241 --> 00:26:21.796
and I was angry about it, but it worked.

00:26:21.796 --> 00:26:22.824
(laughter)

00:26:22.824 --> 00:26:24.503
And so, along the way I realized

00:26:24.503 --> 00:26:29.144
that the candor, the
authenticity, the vulnerability,

00:26:29.144 --> 00:26:33.790
is what actually attracts
the other people to you.

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:35.386
The insecurity roots you in this place

00:26:35.386 --> 00:26:38.930
of showing you're impervious
that you've got all the answers

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:41.987
but what that does is it
renders everyone around you

00:26:41.987 --> 00:26:44.947
helpless, they're just like,
I'm not valued here, right?

00:26:44.947 --> 00:26:46.599
And when you start to admit that, like,

00:26:46.599 --> 00:26:49.191
I don't know what I'm
doing, I need some help,

00:26:49.191 --> 00:26:51.517
then people start attracting
you and working with you.

00:26:51.517 --> 00:26:54.229
I mean, I have a principle
that I guide my life by

00:26:54.229 --> 00:26:56.080
and try and teach this
to our entrepreneurs

00:26:56.080 --> 00:26:57.512
called bold humility.

00:26:57.512 --> 00:26:59.239
There are all these
business books that say,

00:26:59.239 --> 00:27:01.410
like, hey, be humble,
be humble, by humble.

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:03.290
But humility is not a natural state

00:27:03.290 --> 00:27:05.767
for a kick-ass human being, right?

00:27:05.767 --> 00:27:09.676
If you're here, you
probably kick a lot of ass

00:27:09.676 --> 00:27:11.749
at something, you're probably
the best at something.

00:27:11.749 --> 00:27:14.430
You have ambition, you're a risk taker,

00:27:14.430 --> 00:27:17.490
you have learned to hack,
you are a good talker,

00:27:17.490 --> 00:27:20.118
you have through the gift of gab

00:27:20.118 --> 00:27:21.495
wound your way into an amazing situation.

00:27:21.495 --> 00:27:23.184
You hustle, hustle, hustle.

00:27:23.184 --> 00:27:26.865
And I feel like it's unnatural
to apologize for that.

00:27:26.865 --> 00:27:27.973
But where we get into trouble,

00:27:27.973 --> 00:27:30.677
is when we feel like
we have to extend that

00:27:30.677 --> 00:27:32.536
to the shit we don't know.

00:27:32.536 --> 00:27:34.266
When we have to feel like
we've got it all covered.

00:27:34.266 --> 00:27:37.883
And so, I think I have
had such a great, like,

00:27:37.883 --> 00:27:41.317
I don't know if I would've
learned this authenticity

00:27:41.317 --> 00:27:43.639
if I didn't have Twitter, and the replies

00:27:43.639 --> 00:27:47.130
being like, that means a lot
to me, go for it, you know.

00:27:47.130 --> 00:27:49.961
And that's why I kind of live
a lot of my life in public.

00:27:49.961 --> 00:27:51.408
Because I feel I draw a lot of strength

00:27:51.408 --> 00:27:55.640
and reassurance from
the public being like,

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:58.456
look that moment of
failure meant a lot to me

00:27:58.456 --> 00:27:59.472
and I identify with it.

00:27:59.472 --> 00:28:00.305
(slam)

00:28:00.305 --> 00:28:02.761
- Tyler for you, you've got
a deal on Cartoon Network

00:28:02.761 --> 00:28:04.650
that's Time Warner, you've
got a deal with Sony,

00:28:04.650 --> 00:28:08.684
you've used this apparatus,
even though you're only 23,

00:28:08.684 --> 00:28:11.392
you've used this existing
system to build up your profile.

00:28:11.392 --> 00:28:13.630
Do you think you could start

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:15.627
literally without any
of this support system

00:28:15.627 --> 00:28:17.111
and create yourself?

00:28:17.111 --> 00:28:18.419
- Yeah, of course.

00:28:18.419 --> 00:28:20.851
Because that's how I started, like,

00:28:20.851 --> 00:28:25.309
the way that I got famous
was me going on Tumblr

00:28:25.309 --> 00:28:28.026
and creating my own website,
and just putting things out

00:28:28.026 --> 00:28:30.064
like that with the strategic,

00:28:30.064 --> 00:28:31.979
you know, it had a strategy to it,

00:28:31.979 --> 00:28:34.092
but putting out music for free,
and that's how it got known,

00:28:34.092 --> 00:28:36.200
and next thing I know, Sony's like, hey,

00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:37.796
we'll give you a bunch of money

00:28:37.796 --> 00:28:39.938
to do an ex and oh, and
we just eat a little bit

00:28:39.938 --> 00:28:42.385
and I've used them to my advantage

00:28:42.385 --> 00:28:44.878
in the same way that
I've used other things,

00:28:44.878 --> 00:28:46.443
you know, to my advantage.

00:28:46.443 --> 00:28:47.605
- [Interviewer] They didn't make you?

00:28:47.605 --> 00:28:48.500
- No, not at all.

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:52.667
You know, so I know how to
do this without, you know,

00:28:53.771 --> 00:28:55.199
those platforms.

00:28:55.199 --> 00:28:58.206
And another thing is, the
fun thing about this is,

00:28:58.206 --> 00:29:02.304
I'm not, we in our squad,
we're not afraid to fail.

00:29:02.304 --> 00:29:05.405
Like, we're not scared of
failing or whatever, so

00:29:05.405 --> 00:29:07.036
we're learning as we go.

00:29:07.036 --> 00:29:08.823
So maybe this doesn't work out,

00:29:08.823 --> 00:29:10.292
but it'll be a learning experience

00:29:10.292 --> 00:29:13.774
and that'll help other people
look at the mistakes we made

00:29:13.774 --> 00:29:15.176
and figure out what they're trying to do.

00:29:15.176 --> 00:29:16.098
(whoosh)

00:29:16.098 --> 00:29:17.449
